{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PJQzo5P8K8Vrxvqz3KJ9AZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// See http://www.imperialviolet.org/binary/critbit.pdf\n2\t//\n3\t// Differences:\n4\t//   - We only have one child pointer. We allocate sibling nodes at the same\n5\t//     time; they lie in adjacent blocks of memory. The child pointer points\n6\t//     to the left child. There is no waste because every node is either\n7\t//     external or has two children.\n8\t//   - We fold strcmp into the crit-bit finder. If there is no crit-bit\n9\t//     then we have a match.\n10\t//   - During lookup, if the key is shorter than the position of the\n11\t//     crit bit in the current node, the path we take is irrelevant.\n12\t//     Ideally, we'd take one of the shortest paths to a leaf (the only\n13\t//     purpose is to get at a string so we can find the true crit bit),\n14\t//     but for simplicity we always follow the left child.\n15\t//     Our code skips a tiny bit of computation by assigning\n16\t//     direction = 0 rather than c = 0 plus some bit twiddling.\n17\t//   - Insertion: while walking down the tree (after we've figured out the crit\n18\t//     bit), we're guaranteed that the byte number of the current node is less\n19\t//     than the key length, so there's no need for special-case code to handle\n20\t//     keys shorter than the crit bit.\n21\t//   - We combine a couple of comparisons. Instead of byte0 < byte1 and then\n22\t//     mask0 < mask1 if they are equal, we simplify to:\n23\t//       (byte0 << 8) + mask0 < (byte1 << 8) + mask1\n24\t//   - Deletion: we can return early if the key length is shorter than\n25\t//     the current node's critical bit, as this implies the key is absent.\n26\t//   - When following child pointers, rather than p->kid + predicate(),\n27\t//     we prefer predicate() ? p->kid + 1 : p->kid, as this is faster on my\n28\t//     system. We can thus relax predicate(): instead of returning 0 or 1,\n29\t//     it's fine if it simply returns 0 or nonzero. This means we can store\n30\t//     the plain bitmask instead of its inversion, and check the bit with\n31\t//     a single AND.\n32\t\n33\t#include <assert.h>\n34\t#include <stdio.h>\n35\t#include <stdint.h>\n36\t#include <stdlib.h>\n37\t#include <string.h>\n38\t#include \"blt.h\"\n39\t\n40\t// Returns the byte where each bit is 1 except for the bit corresponding to\n41\t// the leading bit of x.\n42\tstatic inline uint8_t to_mask(uint8_t x) {\n43\t  // SWAR trick that sets every bit after the leading bit to 1.\n44\t  x |= x >> 1;\n45\t  x |= x >> 2;\n46\t  x |= x >> 4;\n47\t  // Zero all the bits after the leading bit then invert.\n48\t  return x & ~(x >> 1);\n49\t  if (0) {\n50\t    // Alternative that performs better when there are few set bits.\n51\t    // Zero all bits except leading bit with a bit-twiddling trick.\n52\t    while (x&(x-1)) x &= x-1;\n53\t    // Invert.\n54\t    return 255 - x;\n55\t  }\n56\t}\n57\t\n58\t// An internal node. Leaf nodes are described by BLT_IT.\n59\tstruct blt_node_s {\n60\t  unsigned int byte:32;     // Byte # of difference.\n61\t  unsigned int mask:8;      // ~mask = the crit bit within the byte.\n62\t  unsigned int padding:23;\n63\t  // The following bit corresponds to the last bit of the pointer to the key\n64\t  // in the external node, which is always zero due to malloc alignment.\n65\t  unsigned int is_internal:1;\n66\t  struct blt_node_s *kid;\n67\t};\n68\ttypedef struct blt_node_s *blt_node_ptr;\n69\t\n70\tstatic inline blt_node_ptr follow(blt_node_ptr p, char *key) {\n71\t  return key[p->byte] & p->mask ? p->kid + 1 : p->kid;\n72\t}\n73\t\n74\tstruct BLT {\n75\t  struct blt_node_s root[1];\n76\t  int empty;\n77\t};\n78\t\n79\tBLT *blt_new() {\n80\t  BLT *blt = malloc(sizeof(*blt));\n81\t  blt->empty = 1;\n82\t  return blt;\n83\t}\n84\t\n85\tvoid blt_clear(BLT *blt) {\n86\t  void free_node(blt_node_ptr p) {\n87\t    if (!p->is_internal) {\n88\t      free(((BLT_